      "text": "To determine the area of the figure \\(ABCD\\), we need to find the area of the two sectors of the circle and subtract the area of the overlapping region.\n\nFirst, let's find the area of one sector. The formula for the area of a sector of a circle is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of a sector} = \\frac{\\theta}{360^\\circ} \\times \\pi r^2\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\theta\\) is the central angle in degrees and \\(r\\) is the radius of the circle.\n\nFor our problem, the radius \\(r\\) is 12 and the central angle \\(\\theta\\) for each sector is \\(60^\\circ\\). Plugging in these values, we get:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one sector} = \\frac{60^\\circ}{360^\\circ} \\times \\pi \\times 12^2 = \\frac{1}{6} \\times \\pi \\times 144 = 24\\pi\n\\]\nSince there are two sectors, the total area of the two sectors is:\n\\[\n\\text{Total area of the two sectors} = 2 \\times 24\\pi = 48\\pi\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the area of the overlapping region. The overlapping region is a 30-60-90 triangle formed by the two sectors and the radii of the circle. In a 30-60-90 triangle, the sides are in the ratio \\(1 : \\sqrt{3} : 2\\). The height of the triangle (the distance between the two radii) is the length of the leg opposite the 30-degree angle, which is half the length of the side opposite the 60-degree angle. Therefore, the height of the triangle is:\n\\[\n\\frac{12}{2} \\sqrt{3} = 6\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nThe base of the triangle is the length of the side opposite the 60-degree angle, which is:\n\\[\n12\n\\]\nThe area of the triangle is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of the triangle} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 12 \\times 6\\sqrt{3} = 36\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the area of the overlapping region is \\(36\\sqrt{3}\\).\n\nFinally, the area of the figure \\(ABCD\\) is the area of the two sectors minus the area of the overlapping region:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of figure }ABCD = 48\\pi - 36\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the area of figure \\(ABCD\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{48\\pi - 36\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]",
